("p", "Three observations come out of the rows. The first is that the verbs of Romans 5 are the verbs of acts and standings: entered, passed, reigned, came upon, were made, shall be made. The verbs of 1 Corinthians 15 are the verbs of life and body: die, be made alive, sown, raised, borne, shall bear. The second is that in both passages the right-hand column asks for a mechanism that the text does not give. Paul says how the results run and leaves the machinery unspoken. The third is that where the text does limit a group, it does so in the same clause: “they which receive,” “in Christ,” “they that are Christ’s.”"),
